  "main_text": "<think>\nOkay, let's start by understanding what the user needs. Sofia Thomas wants to exchange her T-Shirt because it's too big. She prefers a size smaller and likes the cotton feel, with black color if available. She has two emails, but I need to authenticate her first.\n\nFirst, I need to find her user ID. The policy says to use email first. Let me check both emails. Maybe she used one of them to register. I'll call find_user_id_by_email with both emails. Wait, but the function takes one email at a time. So I should try each email. Let me start with sofia.thomas3019@example.com.\n\nIf that doesn't work, try sofia.thomas3069@example.com. Once I get the user ID, I can check her orders. Then, find the order with the T-Shirt. Need to confirm which order and item ID she's referring to. Since she wants to exchange, the order must be delivered. Check the order status. If it's delivered, proceed with exchange_delivered_order_items.\n\nBut first, I need to authenticate her. Let me call find_user_id_by_email with the first email.\n</think>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"find_user_id_by_email\", \"arguments\": {\"email\": \"sofia.thomas3019@example.com\"}}\n",
